<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Dev/QA guard-rail for ClickHouse self-telemetry.

  Root cause of the 2026-07 50GB disk blow-up: the sampling profiler wrote to
  system.trace_log once per second per thread, and NO system log table had a TTL,
  so nothing was ever purged (trace_log reached 2.6B rows / 38GB on its own).

  This file (a) caps each system log table with a short TTL so they self-purge, and
  (b) sets a modest per-table row ceiling. The profiler itself is disabled in
  clickhouse-disable-profiler.xml (users.d), which stops trace_log at the source.

  These are diagnostic tables only — safe to age out aggressively in dev.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <trace_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <max_size_rows>5000000</max_size_rows>
    </trace_log>
    <text_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <max_size_rows>5000000</max_size_rows>
    </text_log>
    <!--
      metric_log is the one that actually threatens this deployment, and a TTL alone does
      not bound it usefully. MEASURED on dev 2026-09-08 after clearing: ~960MB/day. At the
      3-day TTL it was written with, that settles near 2.9GB — essentially the 3.2GB that
      OOMKilled the server at startup, so the previous setting was on course to reproduce
      the outage it was added after.

      Two changes, attacking different halves:
        - collect_interval_milliseconds 1000 -> 15000. The default writes one row of ~1000
          columns EVERY SECOND. Fifteen-second granularity is still finer than anything
          anyone reads on a dev box, and it is a 15x cut at the source.
        - TTL 3 days -> 1 day. Diagnostic value here is measured in hours.
      Together: ~64MB steady state instead of ~2.9GB.

      Disk was never the binding constraint — the PVC has 167G free. STARTUP MEMORY is.
      ClickHouse loads part metadata for every table before it serves, and that work sits
      OUTSIDE max_server_memory_usage, so a fat system log table OOMKills a 2Gi container
      before it can run the TTL merge that would have shrunk it. Self-feeding: it died in
      13 seconds, every crash logged more, and the table causing the crash grew from the
      crashing. 2,151 restarts over 18 days, and audit-service's own outage sat behind it.
    -->
    <metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 1 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <collect_interval_milliseconds>15000</collect_interval_milliseconds>
    </metric_log>
    <asynchronous_metric_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 1 DAY DELETE</ttl>
        <collect_interval_milliseconds>15000</collect_interval_milliseconds>
    </asynchronous_metric_log>
    <query_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 7 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_log>
    <query_thread_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 3 DAY DELETE</ttl>
    </query_thread_log>
</clickhouse>
